WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:05.050
What is the difference between a series and
a parallel connection of resistors?

00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:11.129
Here we consider a series and a parallel circuit
with three resistors with resistances R1,

00:00:11.129 --> 00:00:13.009
R2 and R3.

00:00:13.009 --> 00:00:17.310
A voltage U is applied to the resistors.

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:21.050
Difference #1:
Look at how the three resistors are connected

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:22.529
to each other.

00:00:22.529 --> 00:00:27.710
In a series circuit, the positive pole of
the voltage source is connected to one single

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:32.719
resistor and the negative pole is also connected
to one single resistor.

00:00:32.719 --> 00:00:36.060
The resistors in a series circuit form a chain.

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:40.930
In a parallel circuit, on the other hand,
you connect the positive pole of the voltage

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:47.600
source to three ends of the three resistors,
and you do the same with the negative pole.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:49.090
Difference #2:

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:54.109
In a series connection, the same current flows
through all three resistors.

00:00:54.109 --> 00:00:59.890
The total current I is therefore equal to
the current I1 through the first resistor,

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:05.830
but also equal to the current I2 and I3 through
the other two resistors.

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:13.130
In a parallel circuit, however, different
currents I1, I2 and I3 flow through the resistors.

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:23.450
The total current I is the sum of the individual
currents: I = I1 + I2 + I3

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:28.360
In a series circuit, you will measure a different
voltage across each resistor.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:31.030
Voltage U1 across the first resistor.

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:35.720
U2 across the second resistor and U3 across
the third.

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:44.310
The total voltage U is the sum of the individual
voltages: U = U1 + U2 + U3.

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:48.770
In a parallel circuit, on the other hand,
you will measure the same voltage across all

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:50.530
three resistors.

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:56.360
The total voltage is therefore equal to the
voltage U1 between the ends of the first resistor,

00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:00.560
but also equal to the voltage U2 and U3.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:02.500
Difference #4:

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:08.440
The total resistance R of a series circuit
is the sum of the individual resistances:

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:13.600
R is equal to R1 + R2 + R3.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:19.180
In a parallel circuit, however, the total
resistance R is not the sum of the individual

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:20.180
resistances.

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:25.830
The following relationship applies here: The
reciprocal total resistance "1 over R" is

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:34.580
the sum of the reciprocal resistances "1 over
R1" + "1 over R2 + "1 over R3".

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:36.080
Difference #5:

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:40.330
For the conductance values of the resistors,
it is the other way round.

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:45.740
The total conductance G of a parallel circuit
is the sum of the individual conductances

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:48.970
G1, G2 and G3.

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:56.000
And in a series circuit, the reciprocal conductance
"1 over G" is equal to "1 over G1" + "1 over

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:58.300
G2" + "1 over G3".

